  ok: 8 structs match the Rust build; core ak_abi_version()=1

M1 through the C ABI, against the validated manifest. Encode is byte
identity; decode is a re-encode to the same bytes AND a field-by-field
comparison against the graph the builder made, because a re-encode alone
passes a decoder that drops a field the encoder also omits.

payload  elements    bytes  encode  decode  value  fwd  rev  note
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
P1.1            4      858  ok      ok      ok       5    4  
P1.2         1000   218121  ok      ok      ok       5    4  
P1.3          300      605  ok      ok      ok       5    4  the absent path

Reading the crossing columns.
  fwd/rev are counted by the HOST, which is the half R5 asks a host for.
  They are CONSTANT in the element count, in BOTH directions, whether the
  payload carries 4 elements or 1,000. The 5 and 4 cover an encode, a decode
  and a re-encode:
    encode     2 forward (ak_encode_*, ak_elem_*)  1 reverse (loop_results)
    decode     1 forward (ak_decode_*)             2 reverse (apply, add_results)
  ResultRaw is a LEAF, so encode hands the whole run over in one ak_elem_*
  call and decode gets it back in one add_results. That is ABI v1's batching
  predicate doing the thing it exists for, on .NET.

